---
import { langForPath } from '../lib/offer';
/**
 * LocationMap — the orientation map near the top of a place page.
 *
 * ONE COMPONENT, twenty-odd pages. Every location tier renders this: the city
 * route, the neighborhood route and both county routes. An iframe pasted into
 * each template is the duplication this codebase has already had to undo in
 * the footer, in the geo filters and in the citation block, so the markup, the
 * URL shape, the accessible name and the reserved box are all decided here.
 *
 * WHAT IT SHOWS, and this is a content decision rather than a technical one.
 * The map is queried by PLACE NAME — "Ferndale, WA" — and never by the
 * company's street address. A town page is about the town, so the town is what
 * a reader needs oriented; dropping our office pin onto two dozen pages would
 * be both wrong for the reader and the same rectangle repeated. The business
 * address has exactly two homes: /contact/ and the LocalBusiness node in the
 * schema graph.
 *
 * WHY THE KEYLESS EMBED. `maps.google.com/maps?q=…&output=embed` needs no API
 * key, no billing account and no per-page quota. The Maps Embed API would give
 * finer control over zoom and map type; it would also make every location page
 * depend on a secret this project does not have and on a key that can be
 * revoked or rate-limited without anyone noticing until the maps go blank. The
 * keyless form is the weaker embed and the stronger dependency, and that trade
 * is the right way round for a static site.
 *
 * PRIVACY — A DELIBERATE TRADE, NOT AN OVERSIGHT. This is a third-party frame.
 * Loading it hands Google the reader's IP address and lets it set its own
 * cookies, which is a real cost and the reason a lot of sites do not embed
 * maps at all. We are accepting it because a service-area page is one of the
 * few places where a map earns its keep — "is this company anywhere near me"
 * is the question the page exists to answer, and a static screenshot cannot be
 * panned out to answer it. `referrerpolicy` below keeps part of the cost
 * bounded. NOTE: the block moved up next to the lead form on 2 Sep 2026, so
 * `loading="lazy"` no longer saves the fetch for most readers — the frame is
 * now near the top and usually in or close to the viewport on arrival. The
 * attribute stays because it still defers the fetch until layout says it is
 * needed, but the privacy trade above is now paid on essentially every visit
 * to a place page rather than only by readers who scrolled to the end. That
 * is a real change to the calculus and whoever revisits it should know it. If the trade is ever
 * revisited, this is the single file to change — delete the iframe here and
 * the maps come off every location page at once. A future reader should treat
 * this paragraph as the decision, not as an accident.
 *
 * `referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade"` sends the full referring URL
 * over HTTPS and nothing at all on a downgrade to HTTP. Google needs an origin
 * to serve the embed; it does not need to be told, in the clear, which of our
 * pages the reader was on.
 *
 * NO LAYOUT SHIFT. The frame sits in an aspect-ratio box that is laid out
 * before the network is touched, so the map arriving changes nothing above or
 * below it. The width/height attributes on the iframe carry the same ratio for
 * anything that has not applied the stylesheet yet. This site has a
 * no-layout-shift rule and a lazily-loaded third-party frame is the classic
 * way to break it.
 *
 * NOT CHECKED BY THE HARNESS, AND NOT VERIFIABLE FROM THE BUILD ENVIRONMENT.
 * Check 1b crawls img, script, link and meta references; it does not look at
 * iframes, so a typo in the URL built below fails silently and in production
 * rather than in the gate. Worse, the container this site was built in blocks
 * google.com outright, so the embed could never be loaded even once during
 * development — every one of the 27 URLs was verified by reading it, not by
 * seeing a map. Two-word and hyphenated towns are where that would go wrong
 * first; Mount Vernon and Sedro-Woolley were checked character by character.
 * Look at one town page on the live site before calling the launch done.
 *
 * And see `mapsLink` below: because the endpoint is undocumented and could
 * be withdrawn, the caption carries a plain link to the same place, so the
 * block still works if the frame ever stops loading.
 */
interface Props {
  /**
   * The place to show and to announce, written as a reader would say it:
   * "Ferndale", "Whatcom County", "Fairhaven, Bellingham". It is used for the
   * map query, the visible heading and the iframe's accessible name, so there
   * is one string to get right rather than three that can drift.
   */
  place: string;
  /** Appended to the query so the town resolves in the right state. */
  region?: string;
  /**
   * The name as the page's own language says it, where that differs from the
   * query — a Spanish county page says "condado de Whatcom" while the map is
   * still asked for "Whatcom County". Defaults to `place`; a town name is the
   * same in both languages and never needs it.
   */
  label?: string;
}
const { place, region = 'WA', label = place } = Astro.props;
/* Spanish place pages (from 10 Sep 2026) get the same block in Spanish. The
   place name is never translated — Ferndale is Ferndale on a Spanish page too. */
const lang = langForPath(Astro.url.pathname);

/* encodeURIComponent, not a hand-rolled replace. It is what makes "Mount
   Vernon, WA" come out as Mount%20Vernon%2C%20WA and leaves the hyphen in
   Sedro-Woolley alone — a raw space in a src attribute is the exact typo the
   harness will not catch for us. */
const query = encodeURIComponent(`${place}, ${region}`);
const src = `https://maps.google.com/maps?q=${query}&output=embed`;

/* A PLAIN LINK TO THE SAME PLACE, and it is not decoration.
   `output=embed` is a long-standing but UNDOCUMENTED Google endpoint. It has
   worked for over a decade and it powers embeds on a very large number of
   sites, but Google has never published or committed to it — the supported
   route is the Maps Embed API, which needs a key. So there is a real, if
   small, chance this frame goes blank one day with no warning and no error we
   would see.
   This link is the floor under that. It works whether or not the iframe
   loads, whether or not a reader has third-party frames blocked, and whether
   or not Google keeps the endpoint. If the embed ever does break, the block
   degrades to a heading and a working link rather than to a gray rectangle. */
const mapsLink = `https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&query=${query}`;

/* A real accessible name. An iframe with no title is announced as "iframe",
   which tells a screen reader user nothing about whether to enter it. */
const title = lang === 'es' ? `Mapa de ${label}, Washington` : `Map of ${label}, Washington`;
---
{/* data-boilerplate: a map is a widget, not words the page earned. The
    uniqueness floor is measured on prose and the duplicate-sentence scanner
    flags any 10+ word sentence on three or more pages — this caption is on
    every location page by design, and it is furniture rather than content in
    both senses. Marking it here is the same call the link rails and the CTA
    already make. It also picks up the shared eyebrow heading treatment in §6,
    so the block reads as part of the same set as the rails below it. */}
<section class="mapblock" data-boilerplate>
  {/* Phrased so it stays grammatical for every value `place` takes, including
      the two-part neighborhood form — "Fairhaven, Bellingham on the map"
      reads; "Where Fairhaven, Bellingham is" does not. */}
  <h2>{lang === 'es' ? `${label[0].toUpperCase()}${label.slice(1)} en el mapa` : `${label} on the map`}</h2>
  <figure>
    <div class="mapframe">
      {/* data-src, not src — the frame is started by the script below. */}
      <iframe
        data-src={src}
        id="location-map"
        title={title}
        width="640"
        height="480"
        referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade"
      ></iframe>
    </div>
    {lang === 'es' ? (
      <figcaption>
        Solo para orientarse: el mapa se centra en {label}, no en nosotros.{' '}
        <a href={mapsLink} rel="noopener">Abrir {label} en Google Maps</a>. La
        dirección y el horario de la oficina están en la{' '}
        <a href="/es/contacto/">página de contacto</a>.
      </figcaption>
    ) : (
      <figcaption>
        Orientation only — the map centers on {place}, not on us.{' '}
        <a href={mapsLink} rel="noopener">Open {place} in Google Maps</a>. Our
        office address and hours are on the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.
      </figcaption>
    )}
  </figure>
</section>

{/* THE MAP LOADS ON THE READER'S FIRST INTERACTION — from 10 Sep 2026.

    The paragraph at the top of this file says `loading="lazy"` stopped saving
    the fetch once the block moved up beside the lead form: the frame is near
    the top, so the browser loaded it on arrival. Keystone v2's lab gate then
    measured the cost. Every town and neighborhood template sat at an LCP of
    about 3.0s while every other text template painted in 1.9s, and the map
    was the one thing only those two templates carried: Google's embed is
    megabytes of script competing with the page's own first paint.

    So it starts the way the lead form does — on the reader's first scroll,
    tap or key, when it enters the viewport, or five seconds after load. A
    reader who scrolls sees the map arrive; the first paint no longer waits
    on it. It also narrows the privacy trade described at the top of this
    file: a reader who never interacts with the page no longer hands Google
    their IP address just by arriving. Without JavaScript the frame stays
    empty and the "Open in Google Maps" link beneath it is the map, which is
    exactly what that link is there for. One map per page, so one id. */}
<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    var frame = document.getElementById('location-map');
    if (!frame) return;
    var started = false;
    function start() {
      if (started) return;
      started = true;
      frame.src = frame.getAttribute('data-src');
    }
    ['scroll', 'pointerdown', 'keydown', 'touchstart'].forEach(function (ev) {
      window.addEventListener(ev, start, { once: true, passive: true });
    });
    window.addEventListener('load', function () { setTimeout(start, 5000); });
    if (!('IntersectionObserver' in window)) return;
    var io = new IntersectionObserver(function (entries) {
      for (var i = 0; i < entries.length; i++) {
        if (entries[i].isIntersecting) { io.disconnect(); start(); return; }
      }
    }, { rootMargin: '0px' });
    io.observe(frame);
  })();
</script>

{/* No scoped <style> here, deliberately, and for the reason SourceList gives:
    a component that carries its own colors is the one piece of the page the
    tokens cannot reach, and it drifts. Styled in §21a of
    src/styles/global.css. */}
